# than anything a person structured, so a depth finding here is not actionable.
**/routeTree.gen.ts
packages/lexico-entities/src/modules/database/migrations/

# Template sources. These are not compiled as part of any project, and the
# mustache placeholders in their paths are not valid TypeScript identifiers.
configuration/conformetry-templates/

# Planning and prose.
notepads/

# The callidescope examples package. Its fixtures exist to breach the limits —
# deliberately deep stacks, a module that spreads, a callable filed in the wrong
# place — so tracing them here would fail the workspace depth gate for doing
# exactly what they were written to do. They are traced by their own
# configuration instead, gated on report freshness rather than depth:
# `nx run callidescope-examples:callidescope`.
packages/callidescope-examples/

# The four agent-skill packages. Each publishes a toolchain's skill markdown
# and holds barely a callable between them — nothing worth tracing, and no
# real code underneath for a depth number to ever grow into.
packages/callidescope-agents/
packages/codependix-agents/
packages/codometer-agents/
packages/conformetry-agents/

# The codependix, codometer, and conformetry examples packages. Each is a
# fixture corpus built to be read or run, not a library — tracing one reports
# on a corpus's incidental shape rather than on production code. Excluded
# outright rather than left to inherit the workspace default, the same
# reasoning that gives `packages/callidescope-examples` above its own
# report-freshness gate instead of a depth number. This also covers the
# codependix examples package's deliberately unreadable project — its
# `tsconfig.json` names a compiler target TypeScript rejects, which is the
# fixture behind codependix's TypeScript resolution failure example, and it no
# longer needs naming on its own now that the whole package is excluded.
packages/codependix-examples/
packages/codometer-examples/
packages/conformetry-examples/

# The workspace root, as a project of its own. It holds a `tsconfig.json`, so
# discovery finds it, and what it owns is whatever that config's `include`
# catches and no other project claims — the handful of loose maintenance
# scripts under `scripts/` and `.devcontainer/scripts/`. Those are not the
# workspace, and a section describing them is what the root README carried
# until `output.markdown` took that block over. Excluded here rather than left
# in: a project named by its root-relative path has no name at all when that
# path is the root, so its section was headed with empty backticks, and both
# destinations default to the same markers and would fight over the same file.
/tsconfig.json
